
---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "@/components/nav.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/footer.astro";
---

<Layout title="Metasploitable Vulnerability Lab | Alfayo Robin">
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 pt-28 pb-12 text-[var(--white)]">
    <a
      href="/#projects"
      class="mb-8 inline-block text-sm text-[var(--sec)] transition hover:opacity-80"
    >
      ← Back to Projects
    </a>

    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)]">
        Cybersecurity • Vulnerability Assessment
      </p>

      <h1 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-bold md:text-5xl">
        Metasploitable Vulnerability Lab
      </h1>

      <p class="max-w-3xl text-base leading-7 opacity-80 md:text-lg">
        A practical learning environment for understanding vulnerable systems,
        service discovery, and the importance of identifying and reducing
        security weaknesses.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Metasploitable</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">VirtualBox</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Kali Linux</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">Vulnerability Assessment</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">1. Project Overview</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        The objective of this lab was to become familiar with vulnerable
        systems and the process security analysts use to identify potential
        weaknesses. Metasploitable provides a deliberately vulnerable target
        that can be used for safe, controlled security testing in an isolated
        environment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">2. Lab Environment</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li><strong>Virtualization:</strong> Oracle VirtualBox.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing system:</strong> Kali Linux.</li>
        <li><strong>Target:</strong> Metasploitable virtual machine.</li>
        <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Learn service discovery and vulnerability assessment in a controlled lab.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm opacity-70">
        Keep this section accurate to your setup. Add the network mode and
        machine IP addresses only if you have confirmed them, and avoid
        publishing any sensitive information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">3. Setup and Methodology</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li>
          <strong>Prepared the virtual environment.</strong>
          VirtualBox and Kali Linux were already installed. The lab work
          continued with preparing the Metasploitable target machine.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Configured the target.</strong>
          The target was prepared for testing within a controlled virtual
          environment. The network should be isolated from untrusted systems.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Checked connectivity.</strong>
          Confirmed that the testing machine and target could communicate
          within the lab before attempting further assessment.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Explored exposed services.</strong>
          The next assessment step is to identify listening services and
          understand what they reveal about the target. Include the commands
          and results here only if you actually ran them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reviewed potential weaknesses.</strong>
          Observations can be used to understand why outdated software,
          unnecessary services, and weak configurations increase security risk.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">4. Screenshots and Evidence</h2>
      <p class="mb-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        Add your own screenshots to demonstrate the environment and the steps
        you completed. Captions should explain what each image proves.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 1: Metasploitable VM</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            The target virtual machine configured for lab use.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 2: Network connectivity</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            Evidence that the lab systems can communicate, if tested.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 3: Service discovery</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            Record the actual service-discovery output if you performed this step.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <div class="flex min-h-40 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-white/5 p-6 text-center">
            <p class="text-sm opacity-60">Screenshot 4: Assessment findings</p>
          </div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm opacity-75">
            Show verified findings and explain their potential security impact.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">5. Challenges and Considerations</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        Running vulnerable systems requires careful network isolation and
        correct virtual machine configuration. Connectivity problems can
        prevent a security assessment from proceeding, so the virtual network
        and target availability should be checked first. Document the specific
        problems you encountered and how you resolved them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">6. Skills and Lessons Learned</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li>Understanding the purpose of a deliberately vulnerable target.</li>
        <li>Learning how virtual networking supports a controlled security lab.</li>
        <li>Understanding the role of service discovery in vulnerability assessment.</li>
        <li>Connecting technical findings to security risks and remediation.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-6">
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-semibold">7. Conclusion</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        This lab provides a foundation for learning how security weaknesses can
        be investigated in a controlled environment. It also highlights the
        importance of understanding exposed services, verifying findings, and
        applying appropriate security controls. Further testing and documented
        results can build on this foundation as the lab develops.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
